{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":2,"query":"name=Hobsbawm&limit=10&title=Nations and Nationalism since 1780","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"The  "},"title":"construction of nationhood ","subTitle":"ethnicity, religion and nationalism "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Hastings, Adrian.","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Cambridge, U.K"}},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Cambridge University Press"},"dateIssued":"1997"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xii, 235 p. ; 24 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"This is a thorough re-analysis of both nationalism and nations. It challenges the current 'modernist' orthodoxies of such writers as Eric Hobsbawm, Benedict Anderson and Ernest Gellner, and it offers a systematic critique of Hobsbawm's best-selling Nations and Nationalism since 1870. In opposition to books which limit nations and nationalism to the eighteenth century and after, as an aspect of 'modernization', the author argues for a medieval origin to both, dependent upon Biblical religion and the development of vernacular literatures. While theorists of nationhood have paid mostly scant attention to England, the development of the nation-state is seen here as central to the subject, but the analysis is carried forward to embrace many other examples, including Ireland, the South Slavs and modern Africa, before concluding with an overview of the impact of religion, contrasting Islam with Christianity.\"--Back cover."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"The nation and nationalism -- 2. England as prototype -- 3. England's western neighbours -- 4. Western Europe -- 5. The south Slavs -- 6. Some African case studies -- 7. Ethnicity further considered -- 8. Religion further considered."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Adrian Hastings."},"\"This book is based on the Wiles Lectures which I [the author] had the honour to give at The Queen's University of Belfast in May 1996.\"--P. xi.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (pages 210-227) and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":[{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e------"},{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"f------"}]},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Hobsbawm, E. J. (Eric J.),",{"@type":"date","#text":"1917-2012"}]},"titleInfo":{"title":"Nations and nationalism since 1780"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Nationalism","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Nationalism","Religious aspects"]}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"JC311 .H346 1997"},{"@edition":"21","@authority":"ddc","#text":"320.54"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"The 1996 Wiles lectures given at the Queen's University of Belfast"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Wiles lectures"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990075848500203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0521593913 (hardcover)"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780521593915 (hardback)"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0521625440 (pbk.)"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780521625449 (pbk.)"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^97007039^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"36407811"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"h